I use netty as the http server. When the post request body data received by the server is greater than 16k, netty will use DiskAttribute, which will create a temporary file, and use deleteOnExit() when the file needs to be deleted, which results in DeleteOnExitHook.files  keeps getting bigger, and finally leads to memory leaks, oom, etc., I think using DELETE_ON_CLOSE is a correct choice
[click it](https://bugs.java.com/bugdatabase/view_bug.do?bug_id=8193072)
```code
private File tempFile() throws IOException {
        String newpostfix;
        String diskFilename = getDiskFilename();
        if (diskFilename != null) {
            newpostfix = '_' + diskFilename;
        } else {
            newpostfix = getPostfix();
        }
        File tmpFile;
        if (getBaseDirectory() == null) {
            // create a temporary file
            tmpFile = File.createTempFile(getPrefix(), newpostfix);
        } else {
            tmpFile = File.createTempFile(getPrefix(), newpostfix, new File(
                    getBaseDirectory()));
        }
        if (deleteOnExit()) {
            tmpFile.deleteOnExit();
        }
        return tmpFile;
    }
