If DeleteOnExitHook is in the open state and the program runs for a long time, the DeleteOnExitHook file keeps increasing.

In fact, temporary files are also cleaned up when HttpData releases resources, but the records in DeleteOnExitHook will only be cleaned up when the application is stopped.

HttpPostStandardRequestDecoder.java
```java
    /**
     * Destroy the {@link HttpPostStandardRequestDecoder} and release all it resources. After this method
     * was called it is not possible to operate on it anymore.
     */
    @Override
    public void destroy() {
        // Release all data items, including those not yet pulled
        cleanFiles();

        destroyed = true;

        if (undecodedChunk != null && undecodedChunk.refCnt() > 0) {
            undecodedChunk.release();
            undecodedChunk = null;
        }
    }

```

AbstractHttpData.java
```java
     @Override
    public void delete() {
        if (fileChannel != null) {
            try {
                fileChannel.force(false);
            } catch (IOException e) {
                logger.warn("Failed to force.", e);
            } finally {
                try {
                    fileChannel.close();
                } catch (IOException e) {
                    logger.warn("Failed to close a file.", e);
                }
            }
            fileChannel = null;
        } 
        if (!isRenamed) {
            if (file != null && file.exists()) {
                if (!file.delete()) {
                    logger.warn("Failed to delete: {}", file);
                }
            }
            file = null;
        }
    }
```

I re-customized a DeleteOnExitHook hook. If DeleteOnExitHook is turned on, this hook will be added when creating a temporary file. After the request ends and the corresponding resources are released, the current file will be removed from this hook, so that it will not increase all the time.

